{
  "mechanism": {
    "summary": "A gateway builds a key from the API key and route group, then reads two fields from a shared store: token count and last refill time. It adds tokens earned since that time, capped at the bucket's capacity, and lets the request through only if at least one token is available, writing back the new count and time. The read, refill and write run as one atomic script so two gateway nodes cannot both spend the last token. If no token is left, the limiter returns HTTP 429 with Retry-After.",
    "sourceChunkIds": [
      "source-1#chunk-1",
      "source-1#chunk-2"
    ],
    "steps": [
      {
        "text": "Build the limiter key from the API key and the route group.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Read the token count and last-refill timestamp for that key from the shared store.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Add tokens earned since the timestamp (elapsed seconds times the refill rate), capped at the capacity.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "If at least one token is left, take one, write the new count and timestamp back, and let the request through.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Otherwise answer 429 and compute Retry-After from the refill rate.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Run the read, refill and write as one atomic script inside the store so two gateway nodes cannot both spend the same token.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-2"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "plainSummary": "A token bucket holds a fixed number of tokens, which act as permits. It refills them at a steady rate. Each request spends one; an empty bucket means refusal. All front-door servers (gateways) keep the bucket in one shared place, so counts agree. The read, refill and write happen together, with no gap for another node.",
  "analogies": [
    {
      "analogy": "A ticket bucket at a booth: a worker adds tickets at a steady rate, visitors take one each, and the bucket never holds more than its top.",
      "matches": "The bucket's top is capacity, the worker's steady add is refill rate, and each visitor's one ticket is the token a request spends.",
      "breaksDown": "The booth is not a networked shared store, and a refused visitor gets an error immediately rather than waiting for the next ticket."
    }
  ],
  "glossary": [
    {
      "term": "Rate limiter",
      "plainMeaning": "A gatekeeper that decides whether a caller may make a request now.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Token bucket",
      "plainMeaning": "A permit counter that refills at a fixed rate and never exceeds a maximum.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Capacity",
      "plainMeaning": "The maximum number of tokens the bucket can hold.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Refill rate",
      "plainMeaning": "How fast tokens are added back, such as tokens per second.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "429 Too Many Requests",
      "plainMeaning": "The HTTP status returned when the bucket is empty.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Retry-After",
      "plainMeaning": "A response header saying how many seconds until the next token.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Shared store",
      "plainMeaning": "One in-memory service holding all buckets so every gateway sees the same count.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Atomic script",
      "plainMeaning": "One indivisible read-modify-write in the store, so two nodes cannot spend the same token.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Fixed window",
      "plainMeaning": "Counting requests in calendar-minute blocks and resetting at each boundary.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Fail open",
      "plainMeaning": "On store failure, let requests through and enforce limits locally.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-1",
        "source-3#chunk-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Fail closed",
      "plainMeaning": "On store failure, refuse requests rather than serve traffic you cannot count.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2",
        "source-3#chunk-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Local fallback",
      "plainMeaning": "Each instance enforces the limit alone while the shared store is down.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    {
      "id": "fact-1",
      "statement": "A rate limiter answers one question per request: may this caller do this now?",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-2",
      "statement": "The token bucket keeps two numbers per key: the token count and the timestamp of the last refill.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1",
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-3",
      "statement": "A token bucket holds at most a fixed number of tokens (its capacity), refills at a fixed rate, and spends one token per request.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-4",
      "statement": "When the bucket is empty, the request is refused with HTTP 429 Too Many Requests and a Retry-After header giving seconds until the next token.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-5",
      "statement": "At Hollowmere, a free-tier key has a capacity of 100 tokens and a refill rate of 10 tokens per second.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-6",
      "statement": "A client that has been quiet can burst its full capacity at once, then settles to the refill rate.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-7",
      "statement": "The gateway builds the limiter key from the API key and the route group.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-8",
      "statement": "The gateway reads the token count and last-refill timestamp for that key from the shared store.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-9",
      "statement": "It adds tokens earned since the timestamp (elapsed seconds times the refill rate), capped at the capacity.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-10",
      "statement": "If at least one token is left, it takes one, writes the new count and timestamp back, and lets the request through.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-11",
      "statement": "Otherwise it answers 429 and computes Retry-After from the refill rate.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-12",
      "statement": "The read, refill and write run as one atomic script inside the store; without that, two gateway nodes can both see one token left and both let a request through.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-13",
      "statement": "Hollowmere runs 24 gateway nodes behind a load balancer.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-14",
      "statement": "If each node kept its own bucket, a client spread across all of them would get 24 times its limit.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-15",
      "statement": "Keeping the bucket in one shared in-memory store costs a network round trip on every request: 0.4 ms at the median and 2.1 ms at p99 in June 2024 measurements.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-16",
      "statement": "The first version counted requests per calendar minute and reset at the top of each minute; it was cheap, with one counter per key.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-17",
      "statement": "A fixed window let a client send its whole quota in the last second of one minute and again in the first second of the next: up to 2 times the limit inside two seconds.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-18",
      "statement": "The token bucket was chosen because it bounds bursts by capacity no matter where the clock is.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-19",
      "statement": "A rate limiter protects fairness between well-behaved clients, not the service from a real flood.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-20",
      "statement": "A real flood of 2 million requests per second still reaches the gateways, which must parse every request and look up its key before refusing it; dropping it is the network edge's job.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-21",
      "statement": "quotaflow ships token bucket, sliding window log and sliding window counter behind one interface and keeps state in a shared store.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-22",
      "statement": "quotaflow's README claims it is the fastest and most accurate rate limiter, within 0.5% of an ideal limiter under any load.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "low"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-23",
      "statement": "The sliding window log stores every request timestamp in the window and counts them; it is exact but memory grows with the limit.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-24",
      "statement": "At 8 bytes per timestamp, a limit of 1,000 requests per minute costs about 8 KB per active key; 500,000 active keys cost around 4 GB.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-25",
      "statement": "The sliding window counter keeps the current and previous window counts, weighting the previous by overlap; it needs 2 counters per key and is approximate.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-26",
      "statement": "The sliding window counter assumes requests in the previous window were evenly spread, so it can be inexact.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-27",
      "statement": "quotaflow's default algorithm is token bucket.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-28",
      "statement": "quotaflow's default store timeout is 50 ms; a slower store call counts as a store failure.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-29",
      "statement": "By default quotaflow fails closed on store failure: it refuses the request with 503 rather than serving traffic it cannot count.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-30",
      "statement": "Local fallback is off by default; when enabled, each instance enforces the limit on its own while the store is down, multiplying the effective limit by the number of instances.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-31",
      "statement": "Clock skew between instances does not affect quotaflow because timestamps are taken inside the store.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-32",
      "statement": "Keys are not expired until 2 windows after their last request, so memory for idle keys lingers briefly.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-33",
      "statement": "Larkspur Pay's checkout failed for 47 minutes with 31% of checkout requests failing.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-34",
      "statement": "A routine failover moved the limiter's store to a replica in another zone; store calls went from about 1 ms to 80 ms.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-35",
      "statement": "Calls over the 50 ms store timeout counted as failures, and the limiter was configured to fail open, so each of 40 checkout instances enforced the limit locally.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-36",
      "statement": "With 40 instances each allowing the full limit, traffic to the payment provider reached roughly 40 times its contracted rate of 300 requests per second.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-37",
      "statement": "The payment provider began answering 429 to most calls, and checkout errors rose to 31%.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-38",
      "statement": "Fail open was chosen deliberately because refusing payments while the limiter store is slow loses sales on every request.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-39",
      "statement": "That reasoning held for Larkspur's own servers, which had headroom, but not for the downstream provider, whose limit is fixed by contract.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-40",
      "statement": "After the incident, the payment-provider limiter fails closed, local fallback limits are divided by the number of instances, and the checkout store timeout rose to 150 ms.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    }
  ],
